    <!--
      MainScreen's Do Not Disturb banner. Reuses `setup_dnd_missing` for the body
      and `setup_action_allow` for the button, so the wording matches the same
      capability's row on PermissionsScreen exactly; only the title, which a row
      does not need, is its own string.
    -->
    <string name="dnd_banner_title">Do Not Disturb access needed</string>
    <!--
      The setup rows: a title, how the capability stands, and a button carrying
      the verb — but only while there is something left to do. A row whose
      capability is in place says so and offers nothing.

      Each action is a verb the user wants, not the route the tap takes. The old
      copy named the mechanism — `Opens Settings`, `Tap to allow`, `Tap to add`
      — which reads as a description of what is about to happen rather than an
      offer, and left an allowed row still looking like it wanted something.
      Every row says `Allow` and `Allowed`, whether the capability is a runtime
      prompt or, like Do Not Disturb access, a Settings toggle the user goes
      and flips (SPEC.md §5.2) — the mechanism differs but the wording doesn't
      need to. That is the **button**; the status line below it no longer says
      `Allowed` (see next).

      **Every row's status names the capability, in both states** (maintainer,
      2026-09-01; SPEC.md §5.2). `Allowed` answered "did the grant land?", which
      the user already knows because they just did it, and said nothing about
      what it bought — so the screen read as a checklist to clear rather than a
      description of what the app can do. The pair now differs by one word, so
      the row reads the same either way and the difference is the capability
      itself.
    -->
    <string name="setup_dnd_title">Do Not Disturb access</string>
    <string name="setup_dnd_allowed">Snoozes can silence your phone</string>
    <string name="setup_dnd_missing">Snoozes can\'t silence your phone</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_title">Notifications</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_allowed">Snoozes can show status and quick actions</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_missing">Snoozes can\'t show status and quick actions</string>
    <string name="setup_action_allow">Allow</string>
    <!--
      Every setup row's button says the same visible "Allow" (SPEC.md §5.2), so a
      screen reader announcing just that word can't tell a user which permission a
      given button opens once more than one row needs attention at once. This backs
      the accessible name instead — a formatted resource rather than Kotlin string
      concatenation, so a translation can reorder the action and the capability name
      independently of English word order (flagged by Codex on PR #103).
    -->
    <string name="setup_row_action_description">%1$s %2$s</string>
    <!--
      The location row (SPEC.md §3.2). Reuses `setup_action_allow` rather than
      a location-specific verb — "Allow" already says what the user gets, and
      a different verb per permission would be a distinction without a
      difference (AGENTS.md, *Concise copy*). The status line follows the same
      capability pair as every other row — see the note above the Do Not
      Disturb strings — so the wording still never depends on which mechanism
      grants it.
    -->
    <string name="setup_location_title">Location</string>
    <string name="setup_location_allowed">Snoozes can end when you leave</string>
    <string name="setup_location_missing">Snoozes can\'t end when you leave</string>
    <!--
      The `direct` build has no presence monitor at all (SPEC.md §3.4,
      `PRESENCE_TRACKS_DEPARTURE`), so neither line above is true there: one
      would promise a capability the build lacks, and the other would blame a
      permission that is not what is missing. Shown in both states, because the
      grant is not what stands between the user and this — and the row drops its
      Allow button with it, since a button is a promise that granting would
      help.
    -->
    <string name="setup_location_no_departure">Ending when you leave isn\'t in this build yet</string>
    <!--
      The calendar row (SPEC.md §4.3).

      **The only row whose granted state is not `Allowed`**, deliberately. The
      other three gate the product, so "Allowed" is the whole answer — without
      them there is nothing to say but what breaks. This one blocks nothing: it
      adds the ongoing notification's third action and no more. So both states
      name the same capability and differ only in whether it is there, which
      tells a user what the permission is actually for at the moment they are
      deciding.

      **Both say "offer", and both say "a meeting".** Granting the permission
      does not make a snooze end with a meeting — it adds an `Until 17:00`
      action to the ongoing notification, which the user then taps or ignores.
      And the offered time is not necessarily the meeting they are sitting in:
      `NextMeetings` queries the instances *overlapping* now..cap, which
      includes meetings that have not started, and `MeetingEnd.offerFor` takes
      the earliest qualifying end. Saying "end when your meeting does" claimed
      both an automatic ending and a specific meeting, and neither is what the
      feature does (Codex, PR #171).

      Nothing observes the calendar and nothing triggers itself from it, so
      "your next meeting's end time" — what this line used to say — would still
      be the wrong promise even with "offer" added.
    -->
    <string name="setup_calendar_title">Calendar</string>
    <string name="setup_calendar_allowed">Snoozes can end when your meeting does</string>
    <string name="setup_calendar_missing">Snoozes can\'t end when your meeting does</string>
    <!--
      The one disclosure this app shows (SPEC.md §12): a small dialog before
      the background-location prompt specifically, since that is the
      Play-restricted permission the declaration requires it precede — the
      foreground request has no dialog in front of it. Says what location is
      used for, that it only runs while a snooze is active, and that it never
      leaves the phone.
    -->
    <string name="location_background_rationale_title">Allow all the time</string>
    <string name="location_background_rationale_body">Snoozemo uses this to notice you\'ve left even while it\'s closed, so Do Not Disturb turns off on its own. It only runs while a snooze is active, and stays on your phone — nothing is collected or shared.</string>
    <string name="location_background_rationale_continue">Continue</string>
    <string name="location_background_rationale_dismiss">Not now</string>
